/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/google-ace.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("google-ace", [["ace1",0,1,"Your company wants every Finance project to inherit the same IAM grants and organization policies, while keeping those projects apart from Engineering's. What should you create?",["A folder for Finance under the organization, with the Finance projects moved into it","A separate Cloud Billing account that is used only by Finance projects and their resources","One large Finance project that holds every Finance workload","A label department=finance added to each Finance project"],0,"Folders group projects so IAM allow policies and organization policies set on the folder are inherited by every project in it. A billing account only pays for usage, and labels organize costs but carry no policy.","Resource hierarchy",[null,"A billing account only decides who pays for usage; it carries no IAM grants or organization policies for projects to inherit.","Putting every workload in one project removes the separation between workloads and makes least privilege and quotas harder to manage.","Labels help group costs and filter resources, but IAM and organization policies are never inherited through labels."],null],["ace2",0,1,"A developer created a project with the ID acme-web-prod. The team now wants it to be called acme-web-production everywhere. What is true?",["Both the project ID and the project name can be edited later on the project settings page","The display name can be changed, but the project ID is fixed after creation","The project number can be edited, but the project ID cannot","The ID can be changed after moving the project into a new folder"],1,"The project name is only a display label and can be changed. The project ID is globally unique and permanent, so a new ID means a new project. Project numbers are assigned by Google and can't be edited either.","Creating projects",["Only the display name can be edited; the project ID is permanent once the project exists.",null,"The project number is assigned by Google and can't be edited either, so this gets the permanent identifiers backwards.","Moving a project between folders changes its parent, not its ID; the ID stays fixed for the project's lifetime."],null],["ace3",0,1,"You try to deploy a Cloud Run service in a new project and get an error saying the API has not been used in the project before or is disabled. What should you do?",["Grant yourself the Owner role at the organization node so the API permissions are inherited by the project","Link the project to a second billing account","Enable the Cloud Run Admin API, for example with gcloud services enable run.googleapis.com","Create a custom mode VPC network in the project first"],2,"Most Google Cloud APIs must be enabled per project before use. More IAM power or another billing account doesn't enable the API, and Cloud Run doesn't need a VPC.","Enabling APIs",["IAM permissions don't turn an API on; the error is about the API being disabled in the project, and Owner at the organization is also far too broad.","A second billing account is not possible (a project links to one) and wouldn't enable the API anyway.",null,"Cloud Run doesn't need a VPC to deploy, so creating a network does nothing about a disabled API."],null],["ace4",0,1,"Security requires that nobody in the organization can create service account keys, including project Owners. What should you configure?",["A custom IAM role that leaves out iam.serviceAccountKeys.create","A VPC Service Controls perimeter around every project","A log sink that alerts on key creation events","The organization policy constraint that disables service account key creation"],3,"An organization policy constraint applies to everyone, whatever their IAM roles, and is inherited by all projects. A custom role doesn't stop an Owner, and a log alert only reports keys after they exist.","Organization Policy Service",["A custom role only limits people who are given that role; Owners and other broad roles still include the key creation permission.","VPC Service Controls protects data from being exfiltrated across a perimeter; it doesn't stop IAM service account keys from being created.","A sink alert only reports a key after it has already been created, so it detects instead of preventing.",null],null],["ace5",0,1,"Data residency rules say resources in the EU folder may only be created in EU locations. Which control is the most direct?",["The resource locations organization policy constraint set on the EU folder","An IAM deny policy on compute.instances.create for the EU folder","A budget alert scoped to projects in the EU folder","Firewall rules that block traffic to non-EU regions"],0,"The resource locations constraint (gcp.resourceLocations) limits where location-based resources can be created and is inherited by the folder's projects. A deny policy would block creation everywhere, and budgets and firewalls don't control placement.","Organization Policy Service",[null,"Denying instance creation blocks VMs everywhere in the folder, including in EU regions, and does nothing for other resource types.","Budget alerts watch spending, not where resources are created, and can't block anything.","Firewall rules filter network traffic; they have no control over which region a resource is created in."],null],["ace6",0,1,"Employees are already managed in Microsoft Active Directory. You want the same users and groups to exist for Google Cloud sign-in without re-creating them by hand. What should you use?",["Create each user manually on the project's IAM page","Google Cloud Directory Sync to provision them into Cloud Identity","Create one service account per employee and share keys","Workload Identity Federation configured for every employee"],1,"Google Cloud Directory Sync copies users and groups from Active Directory or LDAP into Cloud Identity or Google Workspace. IAM grants access to identities but doesn't create user accounts, and service accounts are for workloads, not people.","Managing users and groups",["IAM pages grant roles to existing identities; they can't create Cloud Identity user accounts, and manual creation is exactly what must be avoided.",null,"Service accounts are identities for workloads, and sharing their keys among employees breaks accountability and security.","Workload Identity Federation lets external workloads get Google credentials; it isn't a way to provision employee accounts from Active Directory."],null],["ace7",0,1,"Twelve analysts need the same BigQuery roles in three projects, and people join and leave the team often. What is the most manageable way to grant access?",["Grant the roles directly to each analyst's user account in each of the three projects, one by one","Create one service account key and share it with the analysts","Grant the roles to a Google group of analysts and manage the group's membership","Grant the analysts the basic Owner role at the organization level"],2,"Granting roles to a group means joiners and leavers are handled by changing membership, not by editing three IAM policies. Individual grants are hard to maintain, shared keys break accountability, and Owner breaks least privilege.","Granting IAM roles",["Individual grants in three projects must be edited every time someone joins or leaves, which is exactly the maintenance burden to avoid.","A shared key hides who did what, can leak, and gives everyone the service account's access with no per-person control.",null,"Owner at the organization grants full control of every project, far beyond the BigQuery access the analysts need."],null],["ace8",0,1,"A user has roles/viewer on a folder and roles/editor on a project inside that folder. What is their effective access on the project?",["Viewer only, because a grant on a folder always overrides a grant on a project below it","No access, because conflicting grants cancel each other out","Viewer until an organization administrator approves the Editor grant","Editor, because inherited and direct allow policy grants are combined"],3,"IAM allow policies are additive down the hierarchy: effective access is the union of grants on the project and its ancestors. Nothing overrides or cancels a grant except an IAM deny policy.","Resource hierarchy",["IAM allow policies don't override each other by level; grants on the folder and the project are combined.","Grants never cancel each other out; effective access is the union of all allow policy grants.","There is no approval step for IAM grants; a binding takes effect as soon as it is added.",null],null],["ace9",0,1,"A finance employee must link new projects to the company billing account but must not change the billing account's settings or payment details. Which role on the billing account fits best?",["Billing Account Administrator","Billing Account Viewer","Billing Account User","Billing Account Costs Manager"],2,"Billing Account User allows linking projects to the billing account (together with a project-level permission such as Project Billing Manager). Administrator could change settings, Viewer can't link projects, and Costs Manager manages budgets and exports.","Cloud Billing accounts",["Billing Account Administrator can change payment methods and settings, which the employee must not be able to do.","Billing Account Viewer can only view costs and transactions; it can't link projects to the account.",null,"Costs Manager manages budgets and cost exports; it isn't the role meant for linking projects to the billing account."],null],["ace10",0,1,"A startup wants email alerts when its monthly spend reaches 50%, 90% and 100% of $1,000. What should it create?",["A Cloud Monitoring uptime check on the billing console page with an email notification channel","A budget on the billing account with threshold rules at those percentages","A quota limit on each project's API usage","An organization policy that restricts spending"],1,"Cloud Billing budgets send alerts at the thresholds you set, based on actual or forecasted spend. Uptime checks probe endpoints, quotas limit resource counts, and organization policies don't track money.","Budgets and alerts",["Uptime checks test whether an endpoint responds; they don't track spending against an amount.",null,"Quotas limit resource counts or API rates; they don't send alerts at percentages of a dollar amount.","Organization policies restrict configurations such as locations; there is no organization policy that tracks or limits spending."],null],["ace11",0,1,"Which statement about Cloud Billing budgets is correct?",["Budgets send alerts at thresholds but do not cap or stop spending on their own","A budget automatically shuts down all billable resources when the budgeted amount is reached","Budgets can only cover a whole billing account, never selected projects","Budget alerts are only available with a paid Customer Care plan"],0,"Budgets notify; they don't stop resources. To act automatically you publish budget notifications to Pub/Sub and handle them with your own code. Budgets can be scoped to projects, services or labels.","Budgets and alerts",[null,"Budgets never shut down resources by themselves; automatic action requires Pub/Sub notifications and your own code.","Budgets can be scoped to selected projects, services or labels, not only the whole billing account.","Budgets and their alerts are part of Cloud Billing for every account; they don't require a paid support plan."],null],["ace12",0,1,"The finance team wants to analyze detailed daily Google Cloud costs by project and label using SQL. What should you configure?",["A log sink that sends Admin Activity audit logs to BigQuery","Budget notifications published to a Pub/Sub topic","A monthly download of the PDF invoice","Cloud Billing data export to a BigQuery dataset"],3,"Billing export to BigQuery writes detailed usage and cost rows, including project and label, that you can query with SQL. Audit logs record who changed what, not costs, and invoices lack the detail.","Budgets and alerts",["Admin Activity audit logs record who changed configuration, not how much anything cost.","Budget notifications report threshold crossings, not the detailed per-project and per-label cost rows needed for SQL analysis.","A PDF invoice is a monthly summary that can't be queried with SQL by day, project or label.",null],null],["ace13",0,1,"You work in dev-proj (us-central1) and prod-proj (europe-west1) every day and keep running commands against the wrong one. Which gcloud feature helps most?",["Named configurations, created with gcloud config configurations create and switched with activate","A separate Cloud Shell browser tab for each project, remembering which tab points at which project","Adding --quiet to every gcloud command","An organization policy that sets a default region"],0,"Each named configuration stores its own account, project, region and zone, and one command switches between them. --quiet only suppresses prompts, and organization policies don't set CLI defaults.","Configuring the gcloud CLI",[null,"Relying on remembering which tab is which is error-prone and does nothing to set project defaults in the CLI.","--quiet only suppresses interactive prompts; it doesn't change which project a command runs against.","Organization policies restrict resource configurations; they don't set gcloud CLI defaults on your machine."],null],["ace14",0,1,"After installing the Google Cloud CLI on a new laptop, which command walks you through signing in and choosing a default project?",["gcloud auth application-default revoke","gcloud components update","gcloud init","gcloud config list"],2,"gcloud init authorizes an account and creates or updates a configuration with a default project, and optionally a region and zone. components update upgrades the CLI, and config list only shows current settings.","Configuring the gcloud CLI",["This revokes Application Default Credentials used by client libraries; it doesn't sign you in or set a project.","components update upgrades the installed CLI components; it doesn't authorize an account or pick a project.",null,"config list only shows the current configuration's properties; it doesn't walk you through setup."],null],["ace15",0,1,"You need to set the default Compute Engine zone in the active gcloud configuration to us-east1-b. Which command does it?",["gcloud compute zones set us-east1-b","gcloud config set compute/zone us-east1-b","gcloud config set zone/default us-east1-b","gcloud init --zone=us-east1-b"],1,"Defaults are properties set with gcloud config set SECTION/PROPERTY VALUE, so compute/zone is the right property. The other commands don't exist in that form.","Configuring the gcloud CLI",["There is no gcloud compute zones set command; zones are set as a configuration property.",null,"zone/default is not a valid property; the correct section and property are compute/zone.","gcloud init is interactive setup and doesn't take a --zone flag to set the default zone this way."],null],["ace16",0,1,"A contractor must run gcloud and kubectl against your project from a locked-down laptop where they can't install software. What should they use?",["A shared Compute Engine VM with a public IP address and one SSH key that the whole team uses","A GPU-backed Cloud Workstations configuration","A portable copy of the gcloud CLI on a USB drive","Cloud Shell, which runs in the browser with gcloud and kubectl ready to use"],3,"Cloud Shell gives an authenticated browser-based shell with the CLI tools preinstalled and nothing to install locally. A shared VM and key break accountability, and the other options still need software or extra cost.","Cloud Shell and the console",["A shared VM and SSH key break accountability, expose a public IP, and are harder to secure than a per-user browser shell.","Cloud Workstations is a paid managed development environment, and a GPU adds cost for no benefit in running CLI commands.","Running a portable copy from a USB drive still means installing software on a locked-down laptop, which isn't allowed.",null],null],["ace17",0,1,"Creating 40 VMs in us-central1 fails with an error that the CPUS quota is exceeded. What should you do?",["Disable and re-enable the Compute Engine API","Request a higher CPUS quota for us-central1 on the Quotas page","Move the project into a different folder","Switch to Spot VMs, which don't count against any quota"],1,"Compute Engine CPU quotas are per region; you can see usage and request an increase in the console. Re-enabling the API or moving folders doesn't change quotas, and Spot VMs still have quotas.","Quotas",["Re-enabling the API doesn't change the regional CPUS quota that the error says was exceeded.",null,"Folders affect inherited policies, not per-project quotas, so moving the project changes nothing.","Spot VMs also count against quotas (including dedicated preemptible quotas), so this claim is false."],null],["ace18",0,1,"Which statement about the Google Cloud resource hierarchy is correct?",["A project can sit in two folders at once to inherit both sets of policies","Compute and storage resources can be created directly under the organization node without any project","Folders can hold projects and other folders, and each resource belongs to one project","Organization policies can be set only at the project level"],2,"The hierarchy is organization > folders (which can nest) > projects > resources, and each node has exactly one parent. Organization policies and IAM can be set at the organization, folder or project level.","Resource hierarchy",["Every project has exactly one parent, so it can't sit in two folders at once.","Resources such as VMs and buckets always belong to a project; they can't be created directly under the organization.",null,"Organization policies can be set on the organization, folders or projects, not only projects."],null],["ace19",0,1,"A team wants to see which Google Cloud costs belong to each application when several apps share projects. What should they apply to resources?",["Labels such as app=checkout, then group billing reports and exports by label","Network tags such as app-checkout on each VM","A separate billing account for each application, with projects re-linked whenever an app moves","Custom IAM roles named after each application"],0,"Labels are key-value pairs that flow into billing reports and the BigQuery billing export, so costs can be grouped by application. Network tags are for firewall rules and routes, not cost reporting.","Regions, zones and labels",[null,"Network tags are used to target firewall rules and routes; they don't appear in billing data for cost reporting.","Separate billing accounts per app add administrative overhead and don't work when apps share projects.","IAM roles control access and have nothing to do with how costs are attributed in billing reports."],null],["ace20",0,1,"A latency-sensitive app for users in Tokyo must keep running if one zone fails. Which placement fits?",["One VM in a single zone of asia-northeast1","Instances spread across several zones of the asia-northeast1 region","A multi-region Cloud Storage bucket and nothing else","Instances in us-central1 behind Cloud CDN"],1,"Zones are isolated failure domains within a region, so spreading instances across zones in a nearby region (asia-northeast1 is Tokyo) gives low latency and zonal resilience. One zone is a single point of failure, and a distant region adds latency.","Regions, zones and labels",["A single VM in one zone fails completely if that zone has an outage.",null,"A storage bucket can store data across regions but doesn't run the application itself.","us-central1 is in the United States, far from Tokyo users, and CDN caching doesn't make a dynamic app zone-resilient in Asia."],null],["ace21",0,1,"You need to review which principals have which roles on project web-prod. Which command shows the project's IAM allow policy?",["gcloud iam roles list --project=web-prod","gcloud projects describe web-prod","gcloud iam service-accounts list --project=web-prod","gcloud projects get-iam-policy web-prod"],3,"get-iam-policy returns the bindings (roles and members) on the project. roles list shows custom roles defined in the project, describe shows metadata, and service-accounts list shows accounts, not who has access.","Granting IAM roles",["This lists custom roles defined in the project, not who has been granted roles.","describe shows project metadata such as its name, number and state, not its IAM bindings.","This lists the project's service accounts, not the members and roles in its allow policy.",null],null],["ace22",0,2,"A team has a stateless containerized HTTP API with spiky traffic. They want to pay nothing when it's idle and manage no servers or clusters. Which service fits best?",["A Compute Engine managed instance group","A GKE Standard cluster","Cloud Run","Bare Metal Solution"],2,"Cloud Run runs stateless containers, scales with requests and can scale to zero, with no infrastructure to manage. A MIG or GKE Standard cluster keeps nodes running and needs management.","Choosing a compute option",["A MIG needs VMs to be running and managed, so it doesn't scale to zero or remove server management.","A GKE Standard cluster keeps nodes running and requires cluster management, which the team wants to avoid.",null,"Bare Metal Solution provides dedicated physical servers for specialized workloads, the opposite of serverless."],null],["ace23",0,2,"An application needs a specific Linux kernel module and a licensed third-party agent installed on the operating system. Which compute option fits?",["Compute Engine VMs","Cloud Run","App Engine standard environment","Cloud Run functions"],0,"Only Compute Engine gives full control of the OS and kernel. The serverless options run your code in a managed sandbox where you can't load kernel modules.","Choosing a compute option",[null,"Cloud Run runs containers in a managed sandbox where you can't load kernel modules or control the host OS.","App Engine standard runs code in language runtimes with no access to the underlying OS or kernel.","Cloud Run functions run small pieces of code in a managed environment with no control of the OS or kernel."],null],["ace24",0,2,"A batch rendering job can be interrupted and restarted, and lowest cost is the top priority. Which Compute Engine option should you choose?",["Sole-tenant nodes","Spot VMs","A three-year committed use discount","Memory-optimized machine types"],1,"Spot VMs are heavily discounted but can be preempted at any time, which suits fault-tolerant batch jobs. Sole-tenant nodes cost more, and a long commitment doesn't suit an occasional job.","Compute Engine instances",["Sole-tenant nodes give you dedicated hosts for licensing or isolation and cost more than regular VMs, not less.",null,"A three-year commitment suits steady, long-running use; it doesn't fit an occasional interruptible job and still costs more than Spot pricing.","Memory-optimized machines are for large in-memory workloads and are expensive; nothing here needs extra memory."],null],["ace25",0,2,"An application needs 6 vCPUs and 20 GB of memory, and no predefined machine type is close. How do you avoid paying for unused resources?",["Choose the next larger predefined machine type","Use an e2-micro shared-core machine type and let a managed instance group autoscale to cover the load","Attach a local SSD to add memory","Create a custom machine type with 6 vCPUs and 20 GB of memory"],3,"Custom machine types let you choose vCPUs and memory within the family's limits. A larger predefined type wastes money, an e2-micro is far too small, and local SSD is storage, not memory.","Compute Engine instances",["The next larger predefined type means paying for vCPUs or memory the application doesn't need.","An e2-micro is a tiny shared-core machine; scaling out many of them doesn't give one process 6 vCPUs and 20 GB of memory.","Local SSD is block storage, not RAM, so it can't add memory to a VM.",null],null],["ace26",0,2,"A VM needs a scratch cache with very high IOPS and low latency, and losing that data when the VM stops is acceptable. Which storage should you attach?",["Local SSD","A standard persistent disk","A Cloud Storage bucket mounted with Cloud Storage FUSE","A regional persistent disk"],0,"Local SSDs are physically attached to the host for very high performance, but their data doesn't persist through a VM stop or host failure. Network disks are durable but slower, and Cloud Storage is object storage.","Compute Engine disks",[null,"A standard persistent disk is HDD-backed network storage, durable but far slower than what a high-IOPS cache needs.","Cloud Storage FUSE mounts object storage, which has much higher latency than block storage and is a poor fit for a hot cache.","A regional persistent disk adds durability across zones and some write latency, which isn't needed for disposable scratch data."],null],["ace27",0,2,"A VM's data disk must survive a zonal outage so the workload can be restarted in another zone of the same region without losing recent writes. What should you use?",["A local SSD","A regional persistent disk replicated across two zones","A zonal standard persistent disk with a daily snapshot","An ephemeral external IP address"],1,"Regional persistent disks replicate synchronously to two zones in a region, so a VM in the other zone can attach it after a failure. A daily snapshot loses writes since the last snapshot, and local SSD data doesn't survive.","Compute Engine disks",["Local SSD data doesn't survive the VM stopping or a host failure, so it can't be moved to another zone.",null,"A daily snapshot loses every write since the last snapshot, which breaks the no-data-loss requirement.","An IP address has nothing to do with disk replication or preserving data across zones."],null],["ace28",0,2,"Web servers must be recreated automatically when a health check fails, and more VMs must be added as CPU usage rises. What should you create?",["An unmanaged instance group of hand-built VMs","A snapshot schedule on each boot disk","A managed instance group from an instance template, with autoscaling and autohealing","A sole-tenant node group with CPU overcommit and a startup script that restarts the web server process"],2,"Managed instance groups create identical VMs from a template, autoscale on signals such as CPU, and recreate unhealthy VMs through autohealing. Unmanaged groups do neither.","Managed instance groups",["Unmanaged instance groups hold existing VMs but can't autoscale or recreate unhealthy instances automatically.","Snapshot schedules back up disks; they don't add VMs or replace failed ones.",null,"Sole-tenant nodes provide dedicated hosts, and a startup script only runs at boot; neither adds capacity or replaces failed VMs automatically."],null],["ace29",0,2,"You need to change the machine type used by an existing managed instance group. What is the right approach?",["Edit the machine type in the current instance template","Create a new instance template and start a rolling update of the group to it","Stop each VM and change its machine type one by one","Delete the managed instance group and recreate it from the same instance template with more VMs"],1,"Instance templates are immutable, so you create a new template and use a rolling update to replace VMs gradually. Changing VMs by hand drifts from the template, and recreating from the same template changes nothing.","Managed instance groups",["Instance templates are immutable, so the machine type in an existing template can't be edited.",null,"Changing VMs by hand makes them drift from the template, and the MIG may recreate them with the old machine type.","Recreating from the same template gives the same old machine type and causes an outage while the group is rebuilt."],null],["ace30",0,2,"A team wants to run containers on GKE without managing nodes, node pools or node sizes, paying mainly for what pods request. Which option fits?",["GKE Standard with one large node pool","Compute Engine VMs running Container-Optimized OS","GKE Standard with the cluster autoscaler turned off","A GKE Autopilot cluster"],3,"In Autopilot, Google manages the nodes and billing is based mainly on pod resource requests. Standard clusters make you size and pay for nodes, and plain VMs aren't Kubernetes at all.","GKE clusters",["In GKE Standard you still size, manage and pay for the node pool's VMs, which the team wants to avoid.","Plain VMs running Container-Optimized OS aren't Kubernetes and leave all node and scheduling management to you.","Turning off the autoscaler in Standard mode means even more manual node management, not less.",null],null],["ace31",0,2,"A production GKE cluster's control plane must stay available if one zone fails. What should you create?",["A regional cluster, which replicates the control plane across zones","A zonal cluster with a larger node pool","A zonal cluster with node auto-repair enabled","A zonal cluster with a private control plane endpoint"],0,"Regional clusters run control plane replicas and nodes across multiple zones in a region. A zonal cluster has a single control plane in one zone, whatever its node settings.","GKE clusters",[null,"A zonal cluster has one control plane in one zone, so a bigger node pool doesn't keep it available if that zone fails.","Node auto-repair fixes unhealthy nodes but doesn't protect the single zonal control plane from a zone outage.","A private endpoint changes how the control plane is reached, not how many zones it runs in."],null],["ace32",0,2,"Some workloads in a GKE Standard cluster need GPUs, while others run fine on general-purpose machines. What should you do?",["Replace every node in the cluster with GPU machines","Create a second cluster in another project","Add a GPU node pool and target it with node selectors or taints and tolerations","Run the GPU pods as a DaemonSet"],2,"Node pools let one cluster mix machine types, and scheduling controls send GPU pods to the GPU pool. Making every node a GPU node wastes money, and a DaemonSet runs a pod on every node.","GKE clusters",["Making every node a GPU node wastes money on workloads that don't need GPUs.","A second cluster in another project adds management overhead when a node pool in the same cluster solves this.",null,"A DaemonSet runs one pod on every node, which doesn't target GPU machines and isn't how you schedule selected workloads."],null],["ace33",0,2,"You created a Deployment named web whose containers listen on port 8080. Which command exposes it to the internet on port 80 through an external load balancer?",["kubectl expose deployment web --type=ClusterIP --port=80 --target-port=8080 --name=web-public","kubectl expose deployment web --type=LoadBalancer --port=80 --target-port=8080","kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=3","kubectl port-forward deployment/web 80:8080"],1,"A Service of type LoadBalancer makes GKE provision an external load balancer with a public IP. ClusterIP is internal only, and port-forward is a temporary tunnel from your own machine.","Deploying workloads to GKE",["A ClusterIP Service is reachable only inside the cluster, whatever name you give it, so it isn't exposed to the internet.",null,"Scaling changes the number of replicas; it doesn't expose the Deployment on an external IP.","port-forward creates a temporary tunnel from your own machine, not an internet-facing load balancer."],null],["ace34",0,2,"Your team wants to define projects, VPCs and VMs as version-controlled code, preview changes before applying them, and reuse modules. What should you use?",["Commands copied from Cloud Shell history into a wiki","Instance templates exported from the console","Snapshot schedules attached to each disk","Terraform with the Google Cloud provider, using plan and apply"],3,"Terraform describes infrastructure declaratively, shows a plan of changes before apply, and supports reusable modules and state. Saved commands aren't declarative, and templates or schedules cover only one resource type.","Infrastructure as code",["Copied commands are imperative, can't show a preview of changes, and aren't reusable modules.","Instance templates describe only VMs; they can't define projects or VPCs or preview changes across them.","Snapshot schedules create disk backups; they don't define infrastructure at all.",null],null],["ace35",0,2,"An image-processing step must run every time a file is uploaded to a Cloud Storage bucket. Which approach needs the least code and infrastructure?",["A Cloud Run function triggered by the bucket's object finalized event","A VM that polls the bucket every minute","A GKE CronJob that lists the bucket every five minutes","A Cloud Scheduler job that runs every hour"],0,"Event-driven functions (through Eventarc) run once per upload with no servers to manage. Polling and scheduled jobs add latency, cost and code that has to track which files were processed.","Cloud Run services and functions",[null,"A polling VM runs and costs money all the time, adds delay, and needs code to track which files were already processed.","A CronJob needs a GKE cluster and still polls, adding delay and code to avoid reprocessing files.","An hourly scheduled job adds up to an hour of delay and has to work out which files are new."],null],["ace36",0,2,"A Cloud Run service should accept requests only from specific internal services that call it with their own service accounts, not from anonymous users. What should you configure?",["Allow unauthenticated invocations and keep the URL secret","Set the maximum number of instances to 1","Require authentication and grant Cloud Run Invoker only to the callers' service accounts","Deploy the service to a region that has no public endpoint and share its URL only with the calling teams"],2,"With authentication required, callers must present an identity token, and only principals with roles/run.invoker can call the service. A hidden URL is not access control, and scaling settings or regions don't restrict callers.","Cloud Run services and functions",["Unauthenticated invocations let anyone on the internet call the service; a secret URL is not access control.","Maximum instances controls scaling and cost, not who is allowed to call the service.",null,"Cloud Run services get a public URL in every region, and sharing the URL carefully is still not access control."],null],["ace37",0,2,"A global retail app needs a relational database with strong consistency, horizontal scaling and multi-region availability. Which product fits?",["Cloud SQL for MySQL","Spanner","Bigtable","Firestore"],1,"Spanner is a horizontally scalable relational database with strong consistency and multi-region configurations. Cloud SQL is regional and scales up, while Bigtable and Firestore are NoSQL.","Choosing a data product",["Cloud SQL is regional and scales vertically and with read replicas; it can't provide strongly consistent multi-region writes at global scale.",null,"Bigtable is NoSQL with no relational schema or SQL transactions across rows as the app requires.","Firestore is a NoSQL document database, not a relational database with SQL transactions."],null],["ace38",0,2,"A regional web app with moderate traffic needs a managed PostgreSQL database that works with existing SQL tools. Which product fits with the least effort?",["Bigtable","BigQuery","Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL","Memorystore for Redis"],2,"Cloud SQL is managed MySQL, PostgreSQL or SQL Server for typical transactional apps. BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, Bigtable is NoSQL, and Memorystore is an in-memory cache.","Choosing a data product",["Bigtable is a NoSQL wide-column store and doesn't speak PostgreSQL or support standard SQL tools.","BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, not a transactional database for a web app.",null,"Memorystore for Redis is an in-memory cache, not a durable PostgreSQL database."],null],["ace39",0,2,"An IoT platform ingests millions of time-series writes per second and reads them by device ID and timestamp with very low latency. Which product fits?",["Bigtable","Cloud SQL for MySQL","BigQuery","Filestore"],0,"Bigtable is a wide-column NoSQL database built for massive throughput and low-latency key-based access, such as time series. Cloud SQL can't scale writes like this, and BigQuery is for analytics, not serving.","Choosing a data product",[null,"Cloud SQL scales up rather than out and can't absorb millions of writes per second.","BigQuery is built for analytical scans, not for serving low-latency lookups by key at high write rates.","Filestore provides NFS file shares, not a database for time-series reads and writes."],null],["ace40",0,2,"A company needs encrypted connectivity between its data center and a VPC within days, over the public internet, with dynamic routing. What should it deploy?",["Dedicated Interconnect","Cloud NAT","Direct Peering","HA VPN with a Cloud Router using BGP"],3,"HA VPN creates IPsec tunnels over the internet, and Cloud Router exchanges routes with BGP. Dedicated Interconnect is a private physical connection that takes longer to provision, Cloud NAT is outbound internet access, and Direct Peering reaches Google services, not your VPC.","Hybrid connectivity",["Dedicated Interconnect is a private physical connection that takes much longer to provision and doesn't run over the internet.","Cloud NAT gives private VMs outbound internet access; it doesn't connect a data center to a VPC.","Direct Peering connects to Google's public services such as Workspace, not privately to your VPC.",null],null],["ace41",0,2,"Audit files are written once, read maybe once a year, and must be kept for at least 7 years at the lowest storage cost. Which Cloud Storage class fits?",["Standard","Nearline","Coldline","Archive"],3,"Archive has the lowest storage price and a 365-day minimum storage duration, which suits data read less than once a year. Nearline and Coldline cost more to store, and Standard is for frequent access.","Cloud Storage buckets",["Standard has no minimum duration but the highest storage price, which suits frequently accessed data, not a 7-year archive.","Nearline suits data read about once a month and costs more to store than Archive.","Coldline suits data read about once a quarter; Archive is cheaper for data read less than once a year.",null],null],["ace42",0,2,"A bucket should move objects to Coldline 90 days after creation and delete them after three years, automatically. What should you configure?",["Object Lifecycle Management rules on the bucket","A Cloud Scheduler job that runs gcloud storage mv","Object Versioning on the bucket","A retention policy locked with Bucket Lock"],0,"Lifecycle rules can set the storage class and delete objects based on age and other conditions. A retention policy prevents deletion before a time rather than causing it, and versioning keeps old versions.","Cloud Storage buckets",[null,"A scheduled script is extra code to maintain, while lifecycle rules do this natively and reliably.","Versioning keeps old versions of objects; it doesn't change storage classes or delete objects by age.","A retention policy prevents deletion until a time has passed; it doesn't move or delete objects automatically."],null],["ace43",0,2,"Users sometimes overwrite or delete important objects in a bucket, and you need to recover earlier versions. What should you enable?",["Uniform bucket-level access","Object Versioning","Requester Pays","Autoclass"],1,"With Object Versioning, an overwritten or deleted object becomes a noncurrent version that you can restore. The other settings control permissions, who pays for access, and automatic class changes.","Cloud Storage buckets",["Uniform bucket-level access changes how permissions are managed; it doesn't keep previous object versions.",null,"Requester Pays makes the reader pay for access charges; it has nothing to do with recovery.","Autoclass changes storage classes based on access patterns; it doesn't preserve overwritten or deleted data."],null],["ace44",0,2,"You need a VPC where you pick exactly which regions have subnets and which ranges they use, to avoid overlapping on-premises networks in 10.128.0.0/9. Which should you create?",["An auto mode VPC","The default network created with the project","A custom mode VPC with subnets you define","A legacy network"],2,"Custom mode VPCs have only the subnets and ranges you create. Auto mode (and the default network) create a subnet in every region from 10.128.0.0/9, which would overlap here, and legacy networks aren't recommended.","VPC networks",["An auto mode VPC creates subnets in every region from 10.128.0.0/9, which would overlap the on-premises range.","The default network is an auto mode VPC, so it has the same overlapping ranges and a subnet in every region.",null,"Legacy networks are deprecated, have no subnets and aren't recommended for new designs."],null],["ace45",0,2,"VMs with the network tag web must accept HTTP and HTTPS from the internet, and nothing else should be opened. Which firewall rule fits?",["Egress allow tcp:80,443 to 0.0.0.0/0 for all instances","Ingress allow tcp:80,443 from 0.0.0.0/0 with target tag web","Ingress allow all protocols from 0.0.0.0/0 with target tag web","Ingress deny tcp:80,443 from 0.0.0.0/0 at priority 100"],1,"An ingress allow rule for those two ports from any source, scoped by target tag, opens only what's needed. Egress rules control outbound traffic, allowing all protocols is too broad, and a deny rule does the opposite.","VPC networks",["Egress rules control outbound traffic; they don't allow inbound HTTP and HTTPS from users.",null,"Allowing all protocols from anywhere exposes every port on the VMs, which breaks the requirement to open nothing else.","A deny rule blocks exactly the traffic that must be allowed."],null],["ace46",0,2,"A central network team wants to manage one VPC's subnets and firewall rules, while app teams create VMs in their own projects on those subnets. What should they use?",["VPC Network Peering between the network team's VPC and a separate VPC in every application project","Cloud VPN tunnels between each pair of projects","Private Service Connect endpoints","Shared VPC, with a host project and attached service projects"],3,"Shared VPC lets service projects use subnets from a host project whose network is administered centrally. Peering connects separately managed VPCs, and VPN or Private Service Connect don't share subnets.","VPC networks",["Peering connects VPCs that are still administered separately, so each app team would manage its own network instead of the central team.","VPN tunnels between projects are complex, add cost and still leave every project with its own separately managed network.","Private Service Connect exposes specific services privately; it doesn't let teams create VMs in a centrally managed subnet.",null],null],["ace47",0,2,"A global HTTPS web app needs one anycast IP address, URL-based routing to different backends and Cloud CDN. Which load balancer fits?",["Global external Application Load Balancer","Internal passthrough Network Load Balancer","External passthrough Network Load Balancer","Regional internal proxy Network Load Balancer"],0,"The global external Application Load Balancer is a layer 7 proxy with one global anycast IP, URL maps and Cloud CDN integration. Network load balancers work at layer 4 without URL routing, and internal ones aren't internet-facing.","Load balancing",[null,"An internal passthrough Network Load Balancer serves traffic inside a VPC and can't give a public anycast IP or URL routing.","External passthrough Network Load Balancers are regional layer 4 balancers with no URL-based routing or Cloud CDN integration.","An internal proxy Network Load Balancer is regional, internal and layer 4, so it isn't internet-facing and can't route by URL."],null],["ace48",0,2,"VMs with no external IP addresses must download OS updates from the internet but must not be reachable from it. What should you configure?",["An ephemeral external IP on each VM","A global external Application Load Balancer","Cloud NAT on a Cloud Router in that region","VPC Network Peering with the default network"],2,"Cloud NAT gives instances without external IPs outbound internet access while allowing no unsolicited inbound connections. External IPs expose the VMs, and a load balancer handles inbound traffic.","Cloud NAT",["External IPs make each VM directly reachable from the internet, which the requirement forbids.","A load balancer handles inbound requests to backends; it doesn't give private VMs outbound internet access.",null,"Peering with another VPC doesn't provide a path to the internet for VMs without external IPs."],null],["ace49",0,3,"You must SSH to a VM that has no external IP address, without opening port 22 to the internet or running a bastion host. What should you use?",["Cloud NAT on the VM's subnet","gcloud compute ssh with --tunnel-through-iap, and a rule allowing IAP's range 35.235.240.0/20 on tcp:22","Add a temporary external IP to the VM and a firewall rule allowing tcp:22 from 0.0.0.0/0, then remove them","VPC Network Peering with Cloud Shell's network"],1,"IAP TCP forwarding tunnels SSH through Identity-Aware Proxy, so only IAP's range needs to reach port 22 and access is controlled by IAM. Cloud NAT is outbound only, and opening 22 to the world is what you're avoiding.","Managing Compute Engine",["Cloud NAT only handles outbound connections from private VMs; it can't carry an inbound SSH session to them.",null,"Opening port 22 to the whole internet, even temporarily, is exactly what the requirement rules out.","Cloud Shell doesn't live in a VPC you can peer with, so peering can't give it a private path to the VM."],null],["ace50",0,3,"Before a risky OS upgrade on a VM, you want a point-in-time copy of its boot disk that you can restore from. What should you do?",["Create an instance template from the VM","Enable autohealing on the VM","Take a snapshot of the persistent disk","Resize the disk to twice its size"],2,"A snapshot is a point-in-time backup of a disk that you can restore to a new disk. Templates describe configuration, not data, and autohealing recreates VMs without restoring old data.","Snapshots and images",["An instance template stores VM settings such as machine type and image, not the current data on the disk.","Autohealing recreates a VM that fails a health check; it doesn't keep a copy of the disk's current data.",null,"Resizing a disk adds capacity but creates no backup copy to restore from."],null],["ace51",0,3,"You need daily backups of a VM's disks, kept for 14 days, without writing scripts. What should you configure?",["A snapshot schedule attached to the disks","A cron job on the VM that copies files to a bucket","A lifecycle rule on a Cloud Storage bucket","A single machine image created today"],0,"Snapshot schedules create snapshots on a schedule and delete them after the retention period. A cron job is a script to maintain, and a single machine image isn't a recurring backup.","Snapshots and images",[null,"A cron job is a script to write and maintain, which the requirement rules out, and it copies files rather than whole disks.","Lifecycle rules manage objects already in a bucket; they don't create backups of VM disks.","One machine image is a single backup, not daily backups with a 14-day retention."],null],["ace52",0,3,"You built a hardened OS configuration on one VM and want to create many identical VMs from it. What should you create from its boot disk?",["A snapshot schedule","A local SSD","A zonal reservation","A custom image, optionally added to an image family"],3,"Custom images are templates for boot disks, and image families let you always use the latest image in the family. Reservations hold capacity, and a local SSD is scratch storage.","Snapshots and images",["A snapshot schedule backs up disks over time; it isn't the usual template for creating many identical VMs.","Local SSD is temporary scratch storage and can't capture or copy a configured boot disk.","A reservation holds VM capacity in a zone; it contains no OS or software configuration.",null],null],["ace53",0,3,"Pods in a GKE Standard cluster often stay Pending because the default node pool is full. The pool should add nodes automatically, up to 10. Which command enables that?",["kubectl autoscale deployment web --max=10","gcloud container clusters update prod --enable-autoscaling --node-pool=default-pool --min-nodes=1 --max-nodes=10","gcloud compute instance-groups managed set-autoscaling on the managed instance group behind the node pool, with max 10","kubectl scale nodes --replicas=10"],1,"The cluster autoscaler is enabled per node pool with gcloud container clusters update (or node-pools update). kubectl autoscale creates a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler for pods, not nodes, and GKE's node groups shouldn't be autoscaled directly.","Managing GKE",["kubectl autoscale creates a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler that changes pod replicas, not the number of nodes.",null,"GKE manages the instance groups behind node pools, and autoscaling them directly conflicts with GKE; use the cluster autoscaler instead.","There's no kubectl scale for nodes; nodes are VMs managed by GKE, not a scalable Kubernetes workload."],null],["ace54",0,3,"A Deployment named shop runs 3 replicas and must run 8 during a flash sale starting now. Which command does it?",["gcloud container clusters resize shop-cluster --num-nodes=8","kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop","kubectl set image deployment/shop shop=shop:8","kubectl scale deployment/shop --replicas=8"],3,"kubectl scale changes the Deployment's replica count immediately. Resizing the cluster changes nodes, not pods, and rollout undo or set image change versions.","Managing GKE",["Resizing the cluster changes the number of nodes, not how many replicas the Deployment runs.","rollout undo reverts to an earlier revision; it doesn't change the replica count.","set image changes the container image version, and shop:8 is just a tag, not a replica count.",null],null],["ace55",0,3,"After you roll out a new image to the Deployment web, error rates spike. How do you return to the previous version quickly?",["kubectl delete deployment web","gcloud container clusters upgrade prod","kubectl rollout undo deployment/web","kubectl drain the nodes that run web"],2,"rollout undo reverts the Deployment to its previous ReplicaSet revision. Deleting it causes an outage, a cluster upgrade changes the Kubernetes version, and draining just moves pods.","Managing GKE",["Deleting the Deployment removes the app entirely, causing an outage instead of a rollback.","A cluster upgrade changes the Kubernetes version of the cluster, not the app's image.",null,"Draining nodes moves pods elsewhere but they keep running the same faulty image."],null],["ace56",0,3,"You deployed a new revision of a Cloud Run service and want 10% of traffic to go to it while 90% stays on the current revision. What should you do?",["Deploy a second service and use DNS round robin","Use gcloud run services update-traffic to give the new revision 10%","Set the new revision's maximum instances to 10","Delete the old revision after one hour"],1,"Cloud Run can split traffic between revisions of one service by percentage. DNS round robin can't weight traffic precisely, and instance limits don't control traffic share.","Managing Cloud Run",["DNS round robin can't weight traffic precisely and would require running and managing a second service.",null,"Maximum instances caps scaling for the revision; it doesn't set the share of traffic it receives.","Deleting the old revision removes the rollback option and doesn't create a 90/10 split."],null],["ace57",0,3,"Users complain that the first request to a rarely used Cloud Run service is slow because of cold starts. Which setting reduces this?",["Lower the maximum number of instances","Set concurrency to 1","Deploy to a region with fewer users","Set a minimum number of instances above zero"],3,"Minimum instances keep some container instances warm, so requests don't wait for startup (you pay for them while idle). Lowering max instances or concurrency tends to cause more startups, not fewer.","Managing Cloud Run",["Lowering maximum instances limits scaling and can increase queuing; it doesn't keep instances warm.","Concurrency 1 makes each instance handle one request at a time, which causes more new instances and more cold starts.","Region choice affects network latency to users, not container startup time.",null],null],["ace58",0,3,"You need to copy 2 TB of files from an Amazon S3 bucket into Cloud Storage every night, as a managed service. What should you use?",["Storage Transfer Service","gcloud storage cp run from Cloud Shell","Transfer Appliance","BigQuery Data Transfer Service"],0,"Storage Transfer Service moves data from other clouds, HTTP sources or buckets on a schedule. Cloud Shell sessions are temporary, Transfer Appliance is a physical device for offline transfer, and BigQuery Data Transfer Service loads data into BigQuery.","Managing Cloud Storage",[null,"A Cloud Shell session is temporary and interactive, so it isn't a managed, scheduled way to move 2 TB every night.","Transfer Appliance is a physical device for one-off offline transfers, not nightly copies from another cloud.","BigQuery Data Transfer Service loads data into BigQuery tables, not files into Cloud Storage buckets."],null],["ace59",0,3,"Which command lists the objects in the bucket gs://acme-logs?",["gcloud storage buckets create gs://acme-logs","gcloud compute disks list --filter=acme-logs","gcloud storage ls gs://acme-logs","gcloud projects describe acme-logs"],2,"gcloud storage ls lists buckets or the objects in a bucket. The other commands create a bucket, list disks, or describe a project.","Managing Cloud Storage",["This creates a new bucket named acme-logs; it doesn't list any objects.","This lists Compute Engine persistent disks, which are a different storage product from Cloud Storage buckets.",null,"This describes a project named acme-logs, not the contents of a bucket."],null],["ace60",0,3,"A Cloud SQL instance must fail over automatically to another zone if its primary zone goes down. What should you configure?",["A read replica created in the same zone as the primary, promoted by hand during an outage","High availability (regional) configuration with a standby in another zone","Automated backups only","Point-in-time recovery only"],1,"Cloud SQL high availability keeps a synchronously updated standby in a second zone and fails over automatically. Backups and point-in-time recovery need a manual restore, and a same-zone replica doesn't protect against a zone outage.","Operating databases",["A replica in the same zone doesn't help when that zone fails, and manual promotion isn't automatic failover.",null,"Backups require a manual restore to a new or existing instance, so they don't fail over automatically.","Point-in-time recovery restores data to a moment in time; it doesn't keep a standby ready in another zone."],null],["ace61",0,3,"Reporting queries are slowing down a Cloud SQL primary. You want to move read-only traffic elsewhere. What should you add?",["A high availability standby to serve the reads","Longer automated backup retention","A second database user for the reports","A read replica for the reporting queries"],3,"Read replicas serve read-only queries and take load off the primary. The HA standby can't serve traffic, and backup retention or extra users don't add capacity.","Operating databases",["The HA standby only takes over on failover and doesn't serve any read traffic.","Backup retention affects recovery options, not how much query load the primary carries.","An extra database user still runs its queries on the same primary, so the load doesn't move.",null],null],["ace62",0,3,"Before running a large BigQuery query, you want to know how many bytes it will process to estimate on-demand cost. What should you do?",["Add LIMIT 10, which reduces the bytes processed","Do a dry run (bq query --dry_run or the console's query validator)","Export the table to Cloud Storage first","Run SELECT * once to warm the cache"],1,"A dry run validates the query and reports the bytes it would process without charging. LIMIT doesn't reduce bytes scanned on a normal table, and SELECT * scans every column.","Operating databases",["LIMIT only trims the rows returned; BigQuery still scans the full columns of a normal table, so bytes processed don't drop.",null,"Exporting the table costs time and storage and doesn't tell you what the query will process.","SELECT * scans every column, which is the most expensive way to query and doesn't estimate anything."],null],["ace63",0,3,"Subnet 10.10.0.0/24 is running out of addresses, and you need more without recreating the VMs in it. What should you do?",["Shrink the range to /25 and recreate it","Convert the VPC to auto mode","Expand the primary range to a larger prefix such as /22 with expand-ip-range","Create a second VPC and peer it with the first"],2,"gcloud compute networks subnets expand-ip-range can grow a subnet's primary range in place, as long as it doesn't overlap other ranges. Ranges can't be shrunk, and a new VPC doesn't add addresses to this subnet.","Operating networks",["Subnet ranges can't be shrunk, and a /25 would give fewer addresses, not more.","A custom mode VPC can't be converted to auto mode, and auto mode wouldn't enlarge this subnet anyway.",null,"A new VPC doesn't add addresses to the existing subnet, and new VMs would have to be placed in a different network."],null],["ace64",0,3,"A VM's ephemeral external IP changes whenever the VM is stopped, which breaks a partner's allowlist. What should you do?",["Promote the address to a static external IP, or reserve one and assign it","Use only the VM's internal IP address","Put the VM behind Cloud NAT","Create a DNS record for the VM with a TTL of 0 and ask the partner to allowlist the name"],0,"A static external IP stays reserved to your project and doesn't change when the VM stops. You can promote the current ephemeral address to keep it. Cloud NAT is for outbound traffic, and DNS doesn't fix an IP-based allowlist.","Operating networks",[null,"The partner connects over the internet, so the VM's internal IP isn't reachable from their side.","Cloud NAT handles outbound connections from private VMs and doesn't give the VM a fixed inbound address.","An IP-based allowlist compares addresses, not names, so a DNS record doesn't stop the address from changing."],null],["ace65",0,3,"You want an email when average CPU on a group of VMs stays above 80% for 5 minutes. What should you create?",["A log sink that routes the VMs' syslog entries to a Pub/Sub topic read by a mail script","A budget alert at 80%","An uptime check against the VMs","A Cloud Monitoring alerting policy on CPU utilization with an email channel"],3,"Alerting policies watch metric conditions over a duration and notify channels such as email. Uptime checks test availability, budgets track spend, and log sinks route logs.","Cloud Monitoring",["CPU utilization is a metric, not a syslog entry, and a mail script behind Pub/Sub is custom work that alerting policies do natively.","Budget alerts track spending against a budget, not VM CPU usage.","Uptime checks test whether endpoints respond; they don't measure CPU usage over time.",null],null],["ace66",0,3,"You need memory usage and disk space metrics from Compute Engine VMs, which aren't collected by default. What should you install?",["The Cloud Trace client library","The Ops Agent","Cloud Profiler","An uptime check"],1,"The Ops Agent collects guest OS metrics such as memory and disk usage, plus logs. Default VM metrics come from the hypervisor and don't see inside the OS. Trace and Profiler instrument application code.","Cloud Monitoring",["The Cloud Trace library records request latency spans from application code, not OS memory or disk usage.",null,"Cloud Profiler samples CPU and memory use by function in your code, not system-wide memory and disk metrics.","Uptime checks test availability from outside and can't see inside the VM."],null],["ace67",0,3,"You want to be alerted if your public website https://www.example.com stops responding from several locations around the world. What should you configure?",["A log-based metric on the VMs' syslog","A Cloud Trace sampling policy","An uptime check with an alerting policy","Firewall rules logging"],2,"Uptime checks probe a public URL from several global locations, and an alerting policy notifies you when checks fail. Logs and traces only see requests that reach your app.","Cloud Monitoring",["A log-based metric sees only what the VMs log; if the site is down or unreachable, it may produce no logs at all.","Trace sampling controls how requests are traced, not whether the site answers from around the world.",null,"Firewall rules logging records connections allowed or denied by rules; it doesn't test availability from external locations."],null],["ace68",0,3,"Security requires audit logs from every project in the organization to be copied to one BigQuery dataset. What should you create?",["An aggregated sink at the organization level that includes child resources and routes to BigQuery","A sink in each existing project, created by hand, that routes that project's audit logs to the shared dataset","A Monitoring dashboard for the organization","A log-based metric for audit entries"],0,"An organization-level aggregated sink (--include-children) routes matching logs from all projects to one destination, including future projects. Per-project sinks are error-prone and miss new projects.","Cloud Logging",[null,"Per-project sinks must be created by hand and miss any project added later.","A dashboard displays metrics; it doesn't copy log entries anywhere.","A log-based metric counts matching entries but doesn't store the entries in BigQuery."],null],["ace69",0,3,"You need to count how often the text payment declined appears in application logs and chart it over time. What should you create?",["An uptime check","A log bucket with longer retention","A Pub/Sub subscription","A counter log-based metric with a filter for that text"],3,"A counter log-based metric turns matching log entries into a metric that you can chart and alert on. Retention and Pub/Sub don't produce a metric.","Cloud Logging",["An uptime check probes endpoints from outside; it doesn't read log text.","Longer retention keeps logs longer but doesn't turn matching entries into a chartable metric.","A Pub/Sub subscription delivers messages; counting and charting would still require extra tooling.",null],null],["ace70",0,3,"An auditor asks who deleted a Compute Engine VM last week. Where should you look?",["Data Access audit logs, after enabling them","Admin Activity audit logs in Cloud Logging","VPC Flow Logs for the subnet","Cloud Trace spans"],1,"Admin Activity audit logs record calls that change configuration, such as deleting a VM, and are always on. Data Access logs record reads and would need to have been enabled already. Flow logs show network traffic.","Cloud Audit Logs",["Deleting a VM is a configuration change, which Admin Activity logs record; Data Access logs cover reads and aren't needed here.",null,"Flow logs record network traffic samples, not API calls that deleted a VM.","Trace spans show request latency inside applications, not who called the Compute Engine API."],null],["ace71",0,3,"You must record which users read objects in a sensitive Cloud Storage bucket. What must you do?",["Nothing, because Admin Activity logs already record object reads","Turn on VPC Flow Logs for the subnet","Enable Data Access audit logs (DATA_READ) for Cloud Storage","Turn on Object Versioning"],2,"Reading object data is recorded in Data Access audit logs, which are off by default for Cloud Storage. Admin Activity logs cover configuration changes only, and flow logs don't show identities.","Cloud Audit Logs",["Admin Activity logs cover configuration changes, not reads of object data.","Flow logs record network flows and don't show which identity read which object.",null,"Versioning keeps old object versions and records nothing about who read an object."],null],["ace72",0,3,"A Python app on Cloud Run throws occasional exceptions. You want them grouped, counted and alerted on with no extra infrastructure. Which service fits?",["Error Reporting","Cloud Profiler","Cloud Trace","Cloud Scheduler"],0,"Error Reporting groups similar exceptions from logs, counts them and can notify you of new errors. Profiler measures CPU and memory use, and Trace measures request latency.","Troubleshooting tools",[null,"Cloud Profiler shows CPU and memory usage by function, not grouped exceptions with alerts.","Cloud Trace shows request latency across services; it doesn't group or count exceptions.","Cloud Scheduler runs jobs on a schedule and has nothing to do with collecting errors."],null],["ace73",0,4,"A developer needs to start and stop VMs in one project but must not delete them or change IAM. The Editor role is too broad. What should you do?",["Create a custom role with compute.instances.start, stop, get and list, and grant it on the project","Grant the basic Owner role on the project","Grant the basic Viewer role on the project","Grant the predefined Compute Instance Admin (v1) role on the project, since it covers every instance action"],0,"A custom role with only the needed permissions gives least privilege. Owner and Compute Instance Admin also allow deleting VMs (and Owner allows IAM changes), and Viewer can't start or stop VMs.","IAM roles",[null,"Owner allows deleting VMs and changing IAM, the two things the developer must not be able to do.","Viewer is read-only, so the developer couldn't start or stop VMs.","Compute Instance Admin also includes deleting instances, which the developer must not be able to do."],null],["ace74",0,4,"Which statement about the basic roles (Owner, Editor, Viewer) is correct?",["Google recommends them for production because they're simple","Editor includes the permissions needed to view and change the project's IAM allow policy","They're very broad, so predefined or custom roles are preferred for least privilege","They can only be granted at the organization level"],2,"Basic roles include thousands of permissions across services, so Google recommends predefined or custom roles instead. Editor can't manage IAM policies (Owner can), and basic roles can be granted on projects.","IAM roles",["Google recommends predefined or custom roles instead, because basic roles grant far more than most jobs need.","Editor can't view or change IAM policies; that requires Owner or an IAM admin role.",null,"Basic roles can be granted on projects (and other levels), not only the organization."],null],["ace75",0,4,"An app on a Compute Engine VM needs to write objects to one Cloud Storage bucket. What is the recommended way to give it access?",["Create a service account key and copy the JSON file onto the VM","Attach a dedicated service account to the VM and grant it Storage Object Creator on that bucket","Keep the default Compute Engine service account and its Editor role, since Editor already includes Storage access","Run gcloud auth login on the VM with a developer's account"],1,"An attached, dedicated service account gets short-lived credentials from the metadata server, and a bucket-level role limits it to what it needs. Keys can leak, Editor is far too broad, and user credentials tie the app to a person.","Service accounts",["Key files are long-lived secrets that can leak; an attached service account gets short-lived tokens with no key to manage.",null,"Editor does include Storage access, but it also allows changes to almost everything in the project, which breaks least privilege.","User credentials tie the application to a person and grant everything that developer can do."],null],["ace76",0,4,"A CI pipeline on GitHub Actions needs to deploy to Google Cloud, and security forbids long-lived service account keys. What should you use?",["A service account key stored as a GitHub secret","An API key restricted to the deployment APIs","A personal user account shared with the pipeline","Workload Identity Federation with GitHub's OIDC tokens, impersonating a deploy service account"],3,"Workload Identity Federation exchanges the pipeline's OIDC token for short-lived Google credentials, so no key exists. API keys don't authorize most admin APIs, and shared personal accounts break accountability.","Workload Identity Federation",["A key stored as a secret is still a long-lived key, which the security team forbids.","API keys identify a project for some public APIs but can't authorize deployment actions as an IAM principal.","Sharing a personal account breaks accountability and puts a user's full access in the pipeline.",null],null],["ace77",0,4,"Pods in a GKE cluster must call Cloud Storage as a specific identity, without relying on the node's service account or on keys. What should you use?",["Workload Identity Federation for GKE, granting IAM roles to the pod's Kubernetes service account","Grant the node's service account the Storage Admin role","Create a service account key, store it in a Kubernetes Secret and mount it into the pods as a file","The basic Editor role on the node's service account"],0,"Workload Identity Federation for GKE lets a Kubernetes service account act as an IAM principal (or impersonate a Google service account), so each workload gets only its own access. Node-level roles are shared by every pod on the node.","Workload Identity Federation",[null,"A role on the node's service account is shared by every pod on the node and relies on the node identity, which the requirement rules out.","A key mounted as a Secret is still a long-lived key, which the requirement rules out.","Editor on the node's service account is far too broad and still shares one identity among all pods."],null],["ace78",0,4,"An administrator wants to run gcloud commands as the service account deploy-sa to test its permissions, without creating a key. What should they be granted and use?",["Service Account Key Admin, then create and download a key","Owner on the project, then download the service account's credentials and sign in as the service account","Service Account Token Creator on deploy-sa, then run gcloud with --impersonate-service-account","Viewer on deploy-sa, then use gcloud auth login"],2,"Token Creator lets a principal get short-lived tokens for the service account, which gcloud uses with --impersonate-service-account. No key is created, and each call is logged with both identities.","Service account impersonation",["Creating and downloading a key is exactly what the requirement forbids.","Owner is far too broad, and downloading credentials means creating a key, which isn't allowed.",null,"Viewer on the service account doesn't allow generating tokens for it, and gcloud auth login signs in as a user, not a service account."],null],["ace79",0,4,"A user can create VMs but gets a permission error when attaching the service account app-sa to a new VM. What do they need on app-sa?",["Compute Viewer","Service Account User (roles/iam.serviceAccountUser)","Security Reviewer","Logs Writer"],1,"Attaching a service account lets the VM act as it, so Google requires Service Account User on that account. The other roles don't include iam.serviceAccounts.actAs.","Service accounts",["Compute Viewer is read-only for Compute Engine and doesn't include permission to act as a service account.",null,"Security Reviewer can view IAM policies but can't attach or act as a service account.","Logs Writer only lets an identity write log entries; it doesn't allow using a service account."],null],["ace80",0,4,"Staff must reach an internal web app on Compute Engine over HTTPS based on their identity, without a VPN. What should you use?",["Cloud NAT on the app's subnet","Firewall rules that allow each user's home IP","VPC Network Peering with each user's network","Identity-Aware Proxy on the app's HTTPS load balancer backend"],3,"IAP checks each request's user identity (and optional context) before it reaches the app. IP allowlists are fragile and identity-blind, and Cloud NAT is outbound only.","Identity-Aware Proxy and OS Login",["Cloud NAT provides outbound internet access for private VMs, not identity-based inbound access for users.","IP allowlists don't check who the user is and break whenever someone's home address changes.","Peering connects VPC networks, not individual users' home networks, and doesn't check identity.",null],null],["ace81",0,4,"Linux SSH access to VMs must be controlled by IAM roles and removed automatically when an employee's account is disabled. What should you enable?",["Project-wide SSH keys in metadata","Interactive serial console access","OS Login, with roles such as Compute OS Login","Shielded VM secure boot"],2,"OS Login ties Linux accounts to Google identities and IAM, so disabling the account removes access. Metadata SSH keys stay valid until someone removes them.","Identity-Aware Proxy and OS Login",["Metadata SSH keys stay valid until someone removes them, so disabling an account doesn't cut off access.","The serial console is a troubleshooting tool, not an IAM-controlled way to manage everyday SSH access.",null,"Secure boot protects the boot process from tampering; it doesn't control who can SSH in."],null],["ace82",0,4,"Compliance requires that you control, rotate and can disable the key protecting a bucket's data, while Google still performs the encryption. What should you use?",["Google default encryption with Google-owned keys","A Cloud KMS key set as the bucket's default customer-managed encryption key (CMEK)","Customer-supplied encryption keys that you generate and send along with every read and write request","Uniform bucket-level access"],1,"With CMEK you manage the key in Cloud KMS (IAM, rotation, disabling) and Google uses it to encrypt data. Default encryption gives you no key control, and customer-supplied keys must be sent with every request.","Encryption",["With default encryption Google controls the keys, so you can't rotate on your own schedule or disable them.",null,"CSEK does give control, but Google doesn't store the keys and you must send them with every request, unlike the managed option described.","Uniform bucket-level access controls IAM permissions; it has nothing to do with encryption keys."],null],["ace83",0,4,"An app on Cloud Run needs a database password. Where should it be kept?",["Hard-coded in the container image","In a plain environment variable defined in the deployment YAML committed to the source repository","In a Cloud Storage object readable by allUsers","In Secret Manager, with Secret Accessor granted to the service's service account"],3,"Secret Manager stores secrets encrypted, versioned and access-controlled by IAM, and Cloud Run can expose them as environment variables or files. The other places leak the password to anyone with the image, repo or URL.","Secret Manager",["Anyone who can pull the image can read the password, and changing it means rebuilding the image.","A password in the repository is readable by everyone with repo access and stays in its history.","allUsers makes the object readable by anyone on the internet.",null],null],["ace84",0,4,"A bucket must use only IAM for access control, with object ACLs disabled so permissions are consistent. What should you enable?",["Fine-grained access control","Requester Pays","Uniform bucket-level access","Object Versioning"],2,"Uniform bucket-level access disables ACLs, so only bucket-level IAM applies. Fine-grained is the mode that allows ACLs.","Cloud Storage access",["Fine-grained access is the mode that keeps ACLs, the opposite of what's required.","Requester Pays changes who pays for access charges, not how permissions are controlled.",null,"Versioning keeps old object versions; it doesn't change the access control model."],null],["ace85",0,4,"A customer with no Google account must download one private object during the next hour. What should you provide?",["A signed URL that expires in one hour","Public access on the whole bucket","Storage Object Viewer granted to allUsers on the bucket","A copy of a service account key"],0,"A signed URL grants time-limited access to one object without a Google identity, and the bucket stays private. Public access exposes everything, and sharing keys is unsafe.","Cloud Storage access",[null,"Making the whole bucket public exposes every object to anyone, forever, not just one file for an hour.","Granting allUsers read access makes every object in the bucket public with no expiry.","Sharing a service account key hands over broad, long-lived access and is a serious security risk."],null],["ace86",0,4,"You must make sure no bucket in the organization can be made public, even by mistake. What should you set?",["A label public=false on every bucket","The organization policy constraint that enforces public access prevention","Uniform bucket-level access turned on for every existing bucket in each project of the organization","Object Versioning on every bucket"],1,"Enforcing public access prevention through an organization policy blocks allUsers and allAuthenticatedUsers access on all buckets below it. Labels don't enforce anything, and uniform access still allows public IAM grants.","Cloud Storage access",["Labels are metadata for organizing and billing; they don't enforce anything.",null,"Uniform bucket-level access disables ACLs but still allows IAM grants to allUsers, and it doesn't cover new buckets.","Versioning keeps old versions of objects and doesn't block public access."],null],["ace87",0,4,"A user says they can't read a BigQuery dataset, and you want to see which binding is missing or which policy blocks them. Which tool helps?",["Cloud Trace","Error Reporting","The billing cost table","Policy Troubleshooter"],3,"Policy Troubleshooter checks whether a principal has a permission on a resource and shows which policies grant or deny it. The other tools don't evaluate IAM.","Reviewing access",["Cloud Trace analyzes request latency, not IAM permissions.","Error Reporting groups application exceptions; it doesn't evaluate IAM bindings.","The cost table shows billing data, not who has which permissions.",null],null],["ace88",0,4,"You want to find principals with more permissions than they have used recently and get suggestions for smaller roles. What should you use?",["Budget alerts","VPC Flow Logs","IAM role recommendations from the IAM recommender","Cloud Monitoring uptime checks"],2,"The IAM recommender compares granted permissions with actual usage and suggests removing or replacing roles. The other tools track spend, traffic or availability.","Reviewing access",["Budget alerts track spending, not which permissions principals use.","Flow logs record network traffic, not IAM permission usage.",null,"Uptime checks test availability of endpoints and say nothing about IAM roles."],null],["ace89",0,4,"A service account key was accidentally committed to a public repository. What should you do first?",["Disable and delete the key, then review audit logs for activity by the service account","Rewrite the repository history to remove the commit containing the key, then force-push the cleaned branch","Rename the service account","Change the project's display name"],0,"The key stays valid until you disable or delete it, and copies may already exist, so revoke it first and then look for misuse. Removing the commit doesn't invalidate the key.","Service account keys",[null,"Rewriting history doesn't invalidate the key, and anyone who already copied it can still use it.","Renaming isn't possible for a service account's email, and even a new display name leaves the key valid.","The project's display name has nothing to do with whether the leaked key works."],null],["ace90",0,4,"An external auditor must be able to list resources and view IAM policies across the organization, without being able to change anything. Which role fits?",["Organization Administrator","Owner on every project","Editor at the organization","Security Reviewer at the organization"],3,"Security Reviewer grants permissions to list resources and read IAM policies without making changes. The other roles can modify resources or access.","Reviewing access",["Organization Administrator can manage IAM policies across the organization, which is far more than viewing.","Owner allows full changes to every project, not read-only review.","Editor can modify almost every resource, so the auditor could change things.",null],null]]);
